  "worked_solution": "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-2)(t-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=2.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-2)=(t-2)^{2}.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-2)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)={t}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function using the rule $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}:$\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{{t}^{2}}=\\frac{2!}{{s}^{2+1}}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-2s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)(t-2)^{2}} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
